];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>

<Fragment slot="head-scripts">
  {schemaJsons.map((schema) => (<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />))}
</Fragment>

<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="refrigerator-repair/kenmore-ice-maker-not-working"
  eyebrow="Kenmore Refrigerator Ice Maker · Model Lookup Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="Kenmore Refrigerator Won't Make Ice? First, Find Out Who Built It"
  subtitle="Kenmore is a Sears house brand — the first 3 digits of your model number reveal the real maker, and that decides the ice-maker parts and reset steps. Look it up, then work the water path."
  heroImageTitle="Kenmore Ice Maker Not Working Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST + MAKER TABLE -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · Start here</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Your Kenmore's model number tells you who really built it.</h2>

    <p><strong>Kenmore is a Sears house brand, not a manufacturer — the refrigerators are built by other companies and badged Kenmore. The first three digits of your model number identify the real maker, and that decides which ice-maker parts and reset steps apply. Look up the prefix below. Whoever built it, the water path comes first: check the filter, supply, fill tube, freezer temperature, and the feeler arm before anything else. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <table class="maker-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Model starts with</th><th>Actually built by</th><th>Same diagnosis as</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>106</strong> (also 110, 596, 562)</td><td>Whirlpool <em>(most Kenmore fridges)</em></td><td><a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/whirlpool-ice-maker-not-working/">Whirlpool ice maker</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>253</strong> (also 417)</td><td>Frigidaire / Electrolux</td><td><a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/frigidaire-ice-maker-not-working/">Frigidaire ice maker</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>795</strong> or <strong>796</strong></td><td>LG</td><td><a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/lg-ice-maker-not-working/">LG ice maker</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>363</strong> or <strong>364</strong></td><td>GE</td><td><a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/ge-ice-maker-not-working/">GE ice maker</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>401</strong> or <strong>402</strong></td><td>Samsung <em>(some Kenmore models)</em></td><td><a href="/services/ice-maker-repair/samsung-ice-maker-not-working/">Samsung ice maker</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>Some Kenmore refrigerators are Samsung-built (401/402 prefixes); if yours is one and the ice maker has frosted over, the Samsung guide covers that specific pattern. For fridge ice makers of any brand, the <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/ice-maker-issues/">refrigerator ice maker issues</a> guide is the umbrella; for a standalone unit, use the <a href="/services/ice-maker-repair/">ice maker repair</a> hub.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 WHY IT MATTERS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Why the maker matters for a no-ice fix</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Same badge, different parts and reset.</h2>

    <p>Every Kenmore no-ice repair comes down to the same short list — a water filter, the supply and fill tube, the water inlet valve, and the ice maker assembly. What changes with the maker is the specific part number and the reset procedure. A Whirlpool-built (106) Kenmore uses Whirlpool's modular or optical ice maker and its feeler-arm/reset-button reset; an LG-built (795/796) one uses LG's ice maker and the LG Test button; a GE-built (363) one uses the GE feeler-arm and reset. Ordering the right part and using the right reset the first time depends on reading that model-number prefix — which is why a generic "Kenmore" guess so often misses.</p>

    <p>Find your model number on the rating label inside the fridge or along the door frame, match the first three digits to the table above, and follow the linked guide for your maker's specific parts and reset. Our techs do this identification for you when you call.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES (universal) -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · The no-ice causes, whoever built it</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Work the water path first.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Clogged water filter (most common)</h3>
      <p>Good for about six months across every maker; past that, flow drops and ice tapers off then stops. Replacing an overdue filter is the top fix.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Closed supply, kinked line, or frozen fill tube</h3>
      <p>A partly-closed shutoff, a kinked line, or a frozen fill tube all cut off water to the mold. Confirm the supply and thaw the fill tube gently.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Ice maker off, feeler arm up, or warm freezer</h3>
      <p>An off switch, a raised or stuck feeler arm, or a freezer that isn't cold enough all stop production. These are quick homeowner checks on any Kenmore.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Water inlet valve or ice maker assembly</h3>
      <p>When water is available but no ice forms, the inlet valve or the ice maker itself has failed. The exact part differs by maker, but both are tech diagnoses and replacements.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Work the water path, note your maker.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Change the water filter</strong> if it's older than six months — this fixes many no-ice calls on any Kenmore.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the supply</strong> — shutoff valve open, line not kinked — and thaw a frozen fill tube gently.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm the ice maker is on, the feeler arm is down, and the freezer is cold enough.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Note your model prefix</strong> (first three digits) so you or we can match the right maker, part, and reset.</li>
      <li><strong>Reset it</strong> using the linked maker's steps (power-cycle, feeler-arm cycle, or the maker's button). Give it several hours for the first batch.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> filter, supply, arm, temperature, fill-tube thaw, and a reset are safe homeowner work and fix most no-ice calls. Do not replace the water inlet valve or open the ice maker module — those are electrically live, plumbed-in tech jobs.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When it's a tech job</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Water confirmed, still no ice? We identify the maker and the part.</h2>

    <p>When the filter is fresh, the supply is open, the arm is down, and a reset doesn't restore ice, the fault is the inlet valve or the ice maker — and our techs identify the real maker from your model number, carry the fitting Whirlpool, Frigidaire, LG, GE, or Samsung part, and replace only what failed. Reading the prefix means we bring the right part the first time instead of guessing on a "Kenmore." <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>

    <p>More help: the <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/ice-maker-issues/">refrigerator ice maker issues</a> umbrella, the full <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/">refrigerator repair</a> hub, and the maker-specific guides in the table above. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/refrigerator-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/refrigerator-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/refrigerator-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Kenmore ice maker not working: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Kenmore still not making ice after a new filter? Call today.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</Layout>

